## Recent Developments in Mechanochemical Materials Synthesis by Extrusion
Deborah E. Crawford* and José Casaban
Mechanochemical synthesis, i.e., reactions conducted by grinding solid reactants together with no or minimal solvent, has been demonstrated as an excellent technique for the formation of both organic and inorganic compounds. Mechanochemistry is viewed as an alternative approach to chemical synthesis and is not always considered when developing manufacturing processes of fine chemicals. Here, recent advances are highlighted regarding mechanochemical synthesis, by utilizing a well-developed continuous technique – extrusion, and the advantages it offers to further support its use in the manufacturing of these chemicals. To put this work into context, it is shown how extrusion plays a vital role for manufacturing in the food, polymer, and pharmaceutical industries, and how the research carried out by these respective industrialists provides great insight and understanding of the technique, with the results being applicable in the chemical industry. The synthesis of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) is highlighted herein as an excellent example showcasing the advantages that extrusion provides to the manufacture of these materials, one advantage being the exceptional space time yields (STYs) reported for these processes, at three orders of magnitude greater than conventional (solvothermal) synthesis.
## 1. Introduction
Mechanochemistry involves the grinding of solid reagents to initiate a chemical reaction and form either inorganic or organic materials.[1,2] Research in this area has steadily increased from 1985 as a result of the advantages offered by mechanochemistry, particularly the promotion of quantitative reactions either with little solvent (referred to as liquid-assisted grinding) or even no solvent being present.[3] This is particularly advantageous in the move toward making chemical synthesis more sustainable. Two recent example reviews regarding the mechanochemical preparation of inorganic and organic compounds are those by James et al.[4] and Wang.[5]
Major developments in the preparation of inorganic materials, such as alloys,[6] inorganic oxides,[7] and nanocomposites,[8]
have been made via ball milling. A ball mill is a basic type of grinder, often used to simply reduce the particle size of a material, but which can also be used to instigate chemical reactions between solid reagents. It carries out a similar function to grinding with a mortar and pestle, but in a quantitative and reproducible manner. Important examples of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) such as HKUST-1 and ZIF-8 have been prepared this way, as highly pure, crystalline, porous solids.[9] Additionally, the formation of \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}^{[10]}\) and \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{X}^{[11]}\) bonds as well as metal: mediated organic reactions (including Suzuki and click reactions)[12] have been reported using ball mills. Phosphorus ylide formation followed by the subsequent Wittig reaction provides an example of a successful "one-pot, two step" process by ball milling.[13]
Most of this exploratory research in mechanochemical synthesis in recent
years has involved the use of small vibrational ball mills. Research into the scale-up of these mechanochemical reactions has also been ongoing, focusing primarily on the use of larger planetary ball mills.[14] Researchers have investigated whether these reactions can be scaled up directly, by considering factors such as ball weight and diameter, free volume in the vessel, and substrate volume, amongst others. On moving to the kilogram or tonne scale, industrial ball mills (e.g., horizontal mills), where a cylinder is filled with stainless steel or rubber balls and rotated on its axis, are employed for materials processing,[15] if not actual synthesis. However, ball milling is inherently a batch processing technique, with relatively low rates of production, equipment often undergoing shutdown times, and difficulties in decanting the product, depending on the physical nature of the material (e.g., caked products versus free-flowing powders).[16] Significantly, extrusion techniques have very recently begun to be explored as a remarkably effective alternative to milling to circumvent these problems and to move to continuous, scalable mechanochemical synthesis.
The term extrusion refers to a family of continuous processing techniques in which materials are forced through constrained spaces. These processes typically also involve the intense mixing of materials.[17] Extrusion was first patented by Joseph Bramah, in the late 18th century for the manufacture of lead pipes.[18] Since that time, it has been employed across
a number of industries, including food, polymer, and pharmaceutical manufacturing. More recently, as mentioned above, it has also been found to be effective for conducting solvent-free mechanochemical synthesis, in particular for the formation of co-crystals and MOFs.[1] This article highlights these important recent developments, and places them in the context of the better-known applications of extrusion, such as in the food and pharmaceutical industries, and for polymer processing, (Figure 1).
## 2. Extrusion Methodology
Extrusion is a general term covering a family of processing techniques, including single (SSE) and twin screw extrusion (TSE). A typical extrusion line consists of a feeder (volumetric or gravitational) and a heatable barrel containing either one (SSE) or two (TSE) screws that convey the material along the inside of the barrel and subject it to shearing and mixing forces before its exit (Figure 2). Optionally, a die can be attached at the exit to shape the material as it leaves.[19] The extruder can be easily integrated into a continuous manufacturing process as either one component of a processing line or as the entire line itself.
In SSE, the screw has a continually increasing root diameter, which gradually increases the compressive forces on the
material as it passes along the barrel. In TSE, both screws are modular and move in a mutually co-rotating or a counterrotating manner.[17] The shear applied to the material is a result of the enhanced mixing achieved by the interpenetration of the screws. A broad range of screw profiles can be accessed by interchanging screw segments, such as kneading blocks (which cause aggressive mixing), toothed segments (giving better dispersive mixing), or reverse segments (to increase compressive forces and residence time).
There are several variable extrusion parameters including screw speed, screw profile, feed rate, and temperature. At the same time, various output parameters can be monitored, both
