during and after the extrusion process, including throughput rate i.e., the amount of product (kg) produced per hour, and the residence time – the time required for the material to pass through the extruder (typically a few seconds to a few minutes, depending on screw speed). The space time yield (STY), i.e., the amount of product manufactured, per unit reactor volume, per day \((\mathrm{kg}.\mathrm{m}^{-3}.\mathrm{day}^{-1})\), is an engineering parameter that can be readily calculated as a measure of the overall efficiency of the process. The torque, i.e., the force applied to cause the rotation of the screws, is another important processing parameter that can be monitored. Additionally, extrusion can also be combined with on-line monitoring via e.g., Raman or infrared spectroscopy, which can provide vital information regarding the chemical changes taking place inside the extruder.[20]
The extensive application of extrusion has been supported by developments in theory, simulation, and experiments to validate extrusion-process models. Whilst a detailed treatment of these studies is outside the scope of this article, some aspects will be touched upon briefly here. Most of the work has been conducted in the field of polymer processing, but the results are applicable across a number of industries. A commonly applied model for melt extrusion is the Tadmor model, which could potentially be applied to any process that involves the formation of a melt phase. This model discusses the formation of a melt layer between the solid reagent and the heated barrel. The melt layer is carried through the extruder by the transverse flow in the screw channel, and so material builds gradually at the end of the conveying sections. The Tadmor model has aided polymer processing under flood-fed extrusion conditions.[21]
Furthermore, Booy[22] reported on aspects of screw geometry in a fully wiped twin screw extruder. A set of screws of equal cross section were studied, whose geometries were derived from kinematic principles, taking into consideration the influence of screw diameter surface area, and volume, amongst other parameters. The relative motion of the screws results in a pathway for the transfer of material from one screw to the other so that material does not only travel along the direction of the screw.
Kaylon et al. have published extensively on optimization of the extrusion process, including an experimental study of fill degree and the distribution of the material in the extrusion of a thermoplastic elastomer. It was determined that using either staggered reverse kneading segments or neutral kneading segments (i.e., those with elements positioned mutually at \(90^{\circ}\)) in a screw profile resulted in completely filled screw channels, in which the density distribution within the extruder, which is affected by incorporation of air, varies and is related directly to the pressure profile in the extruder. Neutral or reverse profiles also result in a prolonged residence time, in comparison with the use of forward staggered kneading segments. In the latter case, a partially filled channel is observed. This work has been essential to the optimization of mixing and processing conditions in extruders.[23] The effect of air entrainment was also investigated by Kaylon et al., particularly its effect on the rheology and the density distribution of material in the extruder.[24]
Whilst the above investigations were primarily empirical, 2D and 3D finite element method (FEM) models have also been used to address complex manufacturing and processing tasks.
Some of these studies were carried out by Kaylon et al., particularly regarding simulation of 3D mixing and flow in the extruder.[25] Manas-Zloczower et al. also simulated mixing in both conveying and kneading sections of the extruder,[26] whilst Wright et al. simulated the pressure and velocity profiles in kneading segments.[27] In these simulation studies, aspects considered included flow kinematics, chaos dynamics and residence-time distribution. Helpful outcomes of these modeling studies include validation that rheology of the material in the extruder is subject to the mixing efficiency[25] and that residence times are prolonged when the angle between staggered conveying elements is reduced.[28]
## 3. Current Widescale Application of Extrusion in Manufacturing
## 3.1. Food Manufacturing
Extrusion is a major manufacturing process employed in food production, particularly for its ability to produce shaped goods such as dried pasta.[29] It allows the feeding of extra additives at various points along the extruder barrel, and thus is employed in the processing of bread doughs, where supercritical carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{scCO}_{2}\right)\) is injected at an intermediate stage to produce a highly expanded product.[30] Also, Brennan et al.[31] reported the addition (by TSE) of soluble and insoluble fibers into extruded breakfast cereal products in order to reduce the glycemic index, something which was difficult to achieve using other processes.
"Cooking extrusion" is also commonly employed, in which steam is injected into the extruder to instigate the in situ cooking of food.[32] Here, the moisture content becomes a critical factor in determining the extrudate quality. Research carried out in this context into controlling and managing moisture levels might subsequently be applied to other areas, including the synthesis of fine chemicals, which can involve the formation of water as a by-product of the reaction.
## 3.2. Extrusion in Polymer Manufacturing
Extrusion has also been extensively applied in the processing of polymers, so much so that it is difficult to adequately summarize that body of work here. Detailed studies have been made regarding the pressure in the extruder barrel,[33] the energy, and thermal efficiencies of extrusion processes,[34] extrusion-based additive manufacturing,[35] and the importance of the process settings and screw geometry as touched on above. Similarly, the rheological properties, such as viscosity, elasticity, and hardness of the material being processed, have also been studied in detail in this context.[36]
Specialized batch mixers, e.g., the Haake PolyLab mixer, have been developed by a number of extruder manufacturers to enable investigation of the key process parameters in batch mode, and subsequently apply the findings to continuous extrusion processes. These batch mixers readily enable the investigation of process parameters that are important in extrusion, and how they respond to additives and changes in the rheology
of the material as it is processed. Parameters such as rotor speed (which can be related to the screw speed in an extruder), temperature, torque, and energy input are typically monitored and analyzed. For example, the behavior of poly(vinyl chloride) (PVC) has been investigated in a specialized batch mixer, particularly regarding its melt and degradation characteristics as a function of its formulation.[37]
The manufacturing of polymers and plastics has become highly sophisticated and usually involves more than just the extrusion process itself. In fact, the extrusion of polymers can also be controlled by the nature of the die attached to the exit. The use of a die is often an integral part of the process, determining the nature of the physical form (including shape) of the product for a given application. The nature of the die results in essentially six further types of extrusion; one particularly common type is "blown film extrusion",[38] which is employed for manufacturing of plastic shopping bags (Figure 3). There are several dies designed for this application, including annular, spider, and spiral (known to be very complicated to use).[39] Compressed air is used to expand the cooled plastic tube emerging from the die, and then this tube is stretched in both the transverse and draw directions by rollers, eventually forming a film. For very large sheets or films (known as sheet/ film extrusion), specialist dies have to be employed.[40] Similar to blown film extrusion, is "tubing extrusion" – used primarily for the manufacture of PVC pipes.[41] Highly sophisticated dies used in over-jacketing extrusion, coextrusion, and extrusion coating,[42] allow for additional polymer layers to be extruded simultaneously. These are quite elegant processes, an example of a basic application would be the coating of a wire.
## 3.3. Extrusion in Pharmaceutical Manufacturing
Extrusion has been employed in both the industrial and academic pharmaceutical sectors, in particular, hot melt extrusion (HME), for the development of novel drug-delivery technology,
i.e., tablets, capsules, and films, that can be delivered via oral, transdermal, or transmucosal routes.[43] This involves simply carrying out the extrusion (TSE) process at elevated temperatures, such that one or more components is in the melt phase. The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) established the process analytical technology (PAT) scheme,[44] which encourages, supports, and regulates the use of extrusion in the pharmaceutical industry. More recently, HME has been used for taste masking. For example, Nurofen Meltlets lemon are prepared via HME,[45] the active ingredient being the bitter-tasting ibuprofen, which is successfully masked by the HME process. HME allows the interaction of anionic active substances and the functional groups of positively charged polymers, resulting in the creation of hydrogen bonds, consequently masking the taste of the bitter active pharmaceutical ingredient (API).
HME has also been put forward as an excellent alternative to spray-drying, another commonly used processing technique. Patterson et al. highlighted the advantage of using HME for the preparation of glassy solutions of carbamazepine, dipyridamole, and iodonethacin, as the extruded solutions were found to show higher solubility.[46] Improved dissolution is necessary to increase absorption by the body and thus achieve greater therapeutic efficacy.[47] The poor dissolution of products obtained from spray-drying was attributed to the smaller particle size frequently seen with products obtained by this technique, resulting in the very rapid dissolution of the polymer.[46] Therefore, HME is currently being researched to bring more poorly water soluble APIs to the market, in particular class II (APIs with high permeability but low solubility) compounds.
TSE holds many advantages for the pharmaceutical industry, as highlighted by Andrews et al.[48] and Maniruzzaman et al.[43,44] These include solvent-free, continuous processing, access to several screw profiles, and, particularly, the improvement in component uniformity, which is a result of the intense mixing possible with TSE.[48] Also, APIs do not undergo significant compression during extrusion, and so the API is unlikely to undergo polymorphic change as a result of compressive forces.[48] Due to the high shear forces applied, the overall extrusion temperature may also be reduced, thereby avoiding potential thermal degradation of the API. Furthermore, the addition of extra plasticizers such as poly(ethylene glycol)s is known to enable further reduction in the overall temperature of the process.[49]
There have been two significant developments in pharmaceutical manufacturing as a result of HME: i) matrix miniatablets and ii) films. Firstly, De Brabender et al. describe a matrix mini-tablet system where the mini-matrices provide better controlled drug release, and expensive coating, as needed with mini-tablets and pellets, is not required. Most matrix miniatablets are based on the use of both hydrophilic and hydrophobic materials, which are difficult to combine without the use of TSE. With TSE, matrix mini-tablet preparation can be made into a semi-continuous process, which overcomes problems regarding the scale-up of mini-tablet and pellet production, in addition to the coating process.[50] Secondly, drug-delivery films are currently prepared by solvent casting to produce a uniform, flexible film.[51] HME eliminates the requirement for solvent, whilst producing a high-quality film in a one-step process.
The production of a high-quality film is a result of the thorough dispersion of fine API particles throughout the molten polymer, consequently increasing its bioavailability.[52] To date, films produced by HME have not been commercialized, although the future looks promising.
## 4. Chemical Synthesis by Extrusion
## 4.1. Mechanosynthesis of Co-crystals
The above sections describe processing and formulation of materials by extrusion. The first synthetic chemistry work carried out by TSE was in the formation of co-crystals. A co-crystal is a crystalline structure made up of two or more components in a well-defined stoichiometric ratio, where each component may be either an atom, ion, or molecule.[53] Co-crystals of APIs are of significant interest to the pharmaceutical industry as they can lead to increased solubility and bioavailability, as well as enhanced physical stability and improved mechanical properties compared to pure APIs.[54] They are conventionally prepared by melt crystallization, sublimation, and solution crystallization.[53] Very promising work has been carried out into the synthesis of co-crystals assisted by liquid-assisted grinding (LAG) in ball mills.[54] However, perhaps due to the difficulties in scaling ball-milling processes, this has been largely limited to screening for new co-crystal forms. However, the scalability of TSE makes it very attractive for using mechanochemical synthesis for actual manufacturing of co-crystals.
Co-crystal synthesis by extrusion was initially carried out at high temperatures (HME).[45] However, Nagapudi and Alvarez-Núñez and co-workers, of Amgen, reported the first example of liquid-assisted extrusion for co-crystal synthesis.[53] Some of the systems investigated include caffeine–oxalic acid, nicotinamide–trans cinnamic acid, carbamazepine–trans cinnamic acid, carbamazepine—saccharin, and ibuprofen–nicotinamide. Several authors have reported that the key extrusion parameters for successful co-crystal formation are temperature and degree of mixing; however, it was demonstrated on some occasions that the addition of small amounts of solvent (LAG) was more important than temperature, and was actually the critical factor to achieve full conversion.[53–55]
It has been suggested that co-crystal formation is a result of eutectic mixtures being formed during the extrusion process, rather than the grinding itself. However, when trying to prepare the caffeine–oxalic acid co-crystal, heating at high temperatures (to form a eutectic), but without grinding, did not result in any conversion to the desired co-crystal. However, at \(75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (below the melting point of both components) and using a mixing screw design, full conversion was seen.[53] Therefore, for this co-crystal, it is thought that the mechanism is not based on liquid-phase formation. On the other hand, researchers at Amgen report that for the nicotinamide–transcinnamic acid system, it is critical to reach the eutectic temperature for full conversion to the co-crystal. Full conversion in this case is only observed at \(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is higher than both melting points.[53]
As can be seen, the mechanism of co-crystal formation in an extruder may vary. However, the effects of mixing and
temperature are generally considered to be the vital factors in the synthesis.[53–55] The efficient mixing by TSE improves the surface contact between the components, leading to a successful and more efficient method of preparing these compounds.
## 4.2. Mechanosynthesis of MOFs and Discrete Metal Complexes
Achieving chemical synthesis through TSE has been extended with the recent work published by James et al. in the synthesis of MOFs and discrete metal complexes.[1] Efficient methods for the large-scale manufacture of MOFs are currently sought due to the significant interest in these porous materials for various applications such as gas storage, gas separation, and catalysis. Current MOF synthesis typically involves solvothermal batch processing,[56] with large amounts of solvent and potentially also heat being required. ZIF-8 and HKUST-1, two archetypal and potentially commercially important MOFs, can be prepared electrochemically[57] or in a continuous flow reactor as reported by Lester et al.[58] However, synthesis by TSE has been shown to allow for the continuous manufacturing of three MOFs (ZIF-8, HKUST-1, and Al(fumarate)(OH)) in multi-kg \(\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) quantities, with little or no solvent being required.
The mechanochemical synthesis of HKUST-1[59] and ZIF-8[60] at small scales by ball milling had already been reported prior to this recent work; however, the mechanochemical synthesis of Al(fumarate)(OH) had not. James et al. reported the successful preparation of these materials in a highly crystalline form and with high apparent surface areas, as determined by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface-area analysis.[1]
ZIF-8 and Al(fumarate)(OH) were prepared by TSE in the complete absence of any added solvent, by reacting three equivalents of 2-methylimidazole with 0.2 equivalents of basic zinc carbonate (ligand to metal ratio of 3.2) at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Al(fumarate) (OH) was formed by extruding aluminum sulfate octadecahydrate and fumaric acid in the presence of sodium hydroxide at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure 4). HKUST-1 was synthesized by extruding involves extruding copper (II) hydroxide and trimesic acid in the presence of methanol to obtain a blue–green solid.
PXRD and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) BET analysis were the main characterization techniques used to analyze the quality of the material prepared after activation. PXRD of HKUST-1 and ZIF-8 showed that highly crystalline materials were consistently obtained (Figure 4). \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) BET analysis showed that all three MOFs were highly porous, and no hysteresis was observed in their \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms (suggesting a lack of mesoporosity) (Figure 4).[61] The calculated BET surface areas are given in Table 1, and it can be seen that they are very similar to literature values for these materials obtained by other methods. The corresponding pore sizes (ca. \(0.6–0.9\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) are also in agreement with those published, indicating that TSE is an excellent technique for the synthesis of MOFs.
Excellent process efficiency is indicated by the STYs for these processes, which exceed those previously published for solvent-based MOF synthesis by up to three orders of magnitude (Table 2).[61] These large values are a direct result of the small free volume present in the extruder, the lack of solvents taking up this space, and the rapid reactions that can occur
between neat reagents. This is a very significant result in terms of the manufacture of MOFs.
The use of SSE was also reported for the synthesis of high quality porous ZIF-8 giving \(4~\mathrm{kg}\) of product, per hour. In addition to the synthesis of extended porous MOFs, the synthesis of discrete metal complexes via TSE was also investigated, including Ni(salen), Al(quinolate)3 \(\left(\mathrm{Alq}_{3}\right)\), and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NCS})_{2}(\mathrm{PPh}_{3})_{2}\).[1,62] In all three cases, the reagents were fed in the appropriate stoichiometric quantities and no further work-up was required to purify the final compounds. In each example, microanalysis, PXRD, and NMR spectroscopy indicated that high purity products were obtained.
\(\mathrm{Alq}_{3}\), which has important applications for organic light-emitting diodes (OLEDs), was prepared from the reaction of basic aluminium acetate with three equivalents of 8-hydroxyquinoline.[63] This reaction has already been studied extensively in the ball mill by James and co-workers,[62] under solvent-free conditions, and it is found that a novel \(\mathrm{Alq}_{3}.\mathrm{AcOH}\) solvate is formed initially in a different polymorph to crystals obtained from a solution containing acetic acid. Upon desolvation phase pure \(\alpha\)-\(\mathrm{Alq}_{3}\) was obtained.
## 5. Conclusion
5. ConclusionCurrently, the scope and limitations of mechanochemical synthesis have yet to be fully explored. It is notable however, that some limitations in the use of ball milling (such as lack of temperature control as standard and scalability) can be overcome through the use of extrusion. Extrusion is currently widely applied across a variety of industries for processing materials. It is perhaps unusual to use processing techniques taken from food manufacturing and apply them to the synthesis of high-purity fine chemicals. However, recent development show that screw extrusion is actually a very effective and efficient means to conduct chemical synthesis. The substantial body of research already conducted into TSE for the manufacturing of food, processing of polymers, and pharmaceutical processing can now be collated and used to help understand the fundamentals of chemical synthesis by extrusion.
